// ============================================================================
// ecs.z  --  an entity-component-system and the game loop around it.
// ============================================================================
//
// The second program written against unified.z. json.z was a JSON parser: a
// tree of sums, built once, walked recursively, freed all at once. It found
// the sum projection, `try`, `match`-as-expression and `self`-in-an-instance.
//
// It could not reach five things, and this file was chosen to hit all five:
//
//     1. ARITHMETIC ON USER TYPES.        json.z added no two values of its
//                                         own types. `Vec2 + Vec2` does.
//     2. HETEROGENEOUS STORAGE.           json.z erased exactly one value
//                                         (`ptr<Writer>`) and stored none. An
//                                         ECS is a bag of `ptr<Shape>`.
//     3. CALLABLES STORED IN DATA.        json.z passed one closure to one
//                                         function. An event bus keeps them.
//     4. MUTATION IN PLACE.               json.z only ever built and printed.
//                                         A simulation writes back every tick.
//     5. TEARDOWN OF THE UNKNOWN.         json.z used an arena and never
//                                         freed a node. A world despawns.
//
// Same convention as json.z: everything is unified.z's syntax as-is except
// where marked [P#], which points at a numbered proposal in FINDINGS. [W#]
// marks something that held up and must not be lost. Numbering continues from
// json.z -- wins start at [W8], frictions at 13, proposals at [P17].
//
// ============================================================================


// ============================================================================
// imports
// ============================================================================
//
// `List` and `Map` graduated from json.z into std between the two files; Map
// is unchanged from json.z's open-addressed version.

std   :: import("std")
mem   :: std.mem
io    :: std.io
fmt   :: std.fmt
core  :: std.core

Allocator :: mem.Allocator
Writer    :: io.Writer
List      :: std.list.List
Map       :: std.map.Map
Key       :: std.key.Key
Option    :: core.Option
Result    :: core.Result
Fn        :: core.Fn
Fn2       :: core.Fn2


// ============================================================================
// std.num  --  where the operator story breaks.  FRICTION 13, and [P17].
// ============================================================================
//
// ADOPTED, in a different shape. unified.z spells this as one policy per
// operator -- `Add<A, B>`, `Mul<A, B>` -- each with an `Out: type` hole,
// rather than the homogeneous `Num<T>` plus a `Scale` workaround below. The
// reason is [P24], which arrived at the same time and let the result type stop
// being a parameter; `Scale<V, S>` was this file's evidence that it had to.
// The declarations here are local to this file and are left as written,
// because the argument for the proposal is what they cost.
//
// unified.z NOTE 6 says operators are members with symbolic names, and gives
// two examples, `[]` and `()`. Both take `self`. Written the same way, `+` is
//
//     Vec2 :: (x: f32, y: f32) -> self {
//         + :: (&self, b: Vec2) -> Vec2 { Vec2(self.x + b.x, self.y + b.y) }
//     }
//
// and it fails on the second line of real vector code:
//
//     v * 2.0      ->  v.*(2.0)      fine, a member of Vec2
//     2.0 * v      ->  (2.0).*(v)    a member of f32, which I do not own
//
// UFCS would rescue it -- `*(2.0, v)` resolves a free function in the caller's
// scope -- and that is exactly the rescue NOTE 4 forbids, because a free
// function may never satisfy a shape. So under the current rules `Vec2` can be
// added to itself and cannot be scaled by a number, and no shape can require
// "is addable" of it.
//
// THE ANSWER IS ALREADY IN THE LANGUAGE, and it is unified.z's own test that
// finds it. Ask the question NOTE 1 asks: can there legitimately be two of
// these for one type?
//
//     Two ways for a List to index itself?     No.   -> `[]` is a SHAPE.
//     Two ways to compare two u8?              Yes.  -> `Ord` is a POLICY.
//     Two ways to add two Vec2?                Yes -- saturating, wrapping,
//                                              checked, and for a matrix,
//                                              two multiplications.
//                                                    -> `+` is a POLICY.
//
// So `[]` and `()` take `self` and the arithmetic operators do not, and that
// is not an inconsistency, it is the same test giving two answers. Everything
// awkward falls out at once: a policy is not attached to a type, so `2.0 * v`
// needs nobody to own f32; primitives are pooled instances like any other; and
// NOTE 4 stays intact because these are instances, not extrinsic functions.
//
// [P17] `a + b` is `Num<A>.add(a, b)`, solver-found.

Num :: <T> -> self {
    add: (a: T, b: T) -> T
    sub: (a: T, b: T) -> T
    mul: (a: T, b: T) -> T
    zero: () -> T
}

// The primitive instances are ordinary pooled ones. Nothing is built in.
:: -> Num<f32> {
    add  = (a, b) -> f32 { intrinsic_add_f32(a, b) }
    sub  = (a, b) -> f32 { intrinsic_sub_f32(a, b) }
    mul  = (a, b) -> f32 { intrinsic_mul_f32(a, b) }
    zero = ()     -> f32 { 0.0 }
}

// The mixed-operand case, which is why `Num<T>` alone is not enough: the
// operands have different types and so does the result. Written with three
// compile-time parameters it works, and reads badly at every use. See
// FRICTION 14 -- what it actually wants is an OUTPUT parameter.
Scale :: <V, S> -> self {
    scale: (v: V, s: S) -> V
}

:: -> Scale<f32, f32> { scale = (v, s) -> f32 { v * s } }


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Vec2. A record plus two policy instances. Note that the type declaration is
// one line ([W2] from json.z, still the best line in the language) and that
// NOTHING about Vec2 mentions arithmetic -- the instances are separate, which
// is the whole point of them being policies.

Vec2 :: (x: f32, y: f32) -> self

:: -> Num<Vec2> {
    add  = (a, b) -> Vec2 { Vec2(a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y) }
    sub  = (a, b) -> Vec2 { Vec2(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y) }
    mul  = (a, b) -> Vec2 { Vec2(a.x * b.x, a.y * b.y) }   // componentwise
    zero = ()     -> Vec2 { Vec2(0.0, 0.0) }
}

:: -> Scale<Vec2, f32> { scale = (v, s) -> Vec2 { Vec2(v.x * s, v.y * s) } }

// A named second instance, exactly as `reverse_u8` is a second `Ord<u8>`.
// This is the evidence that `+` had to be a policy: `wrapping` cannot be a
// member of Vec2, because `+` already is one and there is only one of those.
saturating :: -> Num<Vec2> {
    add = (a, b) -> Vec2 { Vec2(clamp_unit(a.x + b.x), clamp_unit(a.y + b.y)) }
    sub = (a, b) -> Vec2 { Num<Vec2>.sub(a, b) }           // delegate; NOTE 7
    mul = (a, b) -> Vec2 { Num<Vec2>.mul(a, b) }
    zero = ()    -> Vec2 { Num<Vec2>.zero() }
}

clamp_unit :: (v: f32) -> f32 { if (v > 1.0) { 1.0 } else { if (v < -1.0) { -1.0 } else { v } } }

length_sq :: (v: Vec2) -> f32 { v.x * v.x + v.y * v.y }

// A generic over any numeric type -- the thing the current draft cannot say at
// all, and the reason [P17] is worth a proposal rather than a workaround.
lerp :: <T, N: Num<T> = ?, S: Scale<T, f32> = ?> (a: T, b: T, t: f32) -> T {
    N.add(a, S.scale(N.sub(b, a), t))
}


// ============================================================================
// ecs.entity
// ============================================================================
//
// A generational handle: the id indexes the storage, the generation says the
// slot has not been recycled underneath you. `Entity` is one line, as `Span`
// and `Error` were in json.z.

Entity :: (id: u32, gen: u32) -> self

// Used as a Map key, so it needs a `Key<Entity>` instance -- which is also the
// only way to ask "are these the same entity", because there is no `==` on a
// user type and [P17]'s `Num` is not the right home for equality. `Key` was
// json.z's [P11]: hash and equals must agree, so they live in one block.
:: -> Key<Entity> {
    hash   = (k) -> u64  { (k.id * 2654435761) ^ (k.gen * 40503) }
    equals = (a, b) -> bool { a.id == b.id && a.gen == b.gen }
}

// FRICTION 15 in one line: `Key<Entity>.equals(a, b)` is what you write at
// every comparison site, and it is not obviously an equality test. `a == b`
// under [P17] would desugar to the same call through an `Eq<T>` policy.
same :: (a: Entity, b: Entity) -> bool { Key<Entity>.equals(a, b) }

NO_ENTITY :: Entity(0, 0)


// ============================================================================
// ecs.store  --  a sparse set. Dense array for iteration, map for lookup.
// ============================================================================
//
// This is the container that makes an ECS fast, and writing it found the two
// sharpest results in the file: FRICTION 16 (`[]` cannot be assigned through)
// and FRICTION 17 (a container cannot be encapsulated, because reaching into
// its fields is the only way to mutate an element).

Store :: <C, M: Allocator> (mem: M) -> self {

    dense := List<C, M>(mem)          // the components themselves
    owner := List<Entity, M>(mem)     // dense[i] belongs to owner[i]
    index := Map<Entity, u32, M>(mem) // entity -> dense index

    count :: (&self) -> u32 { self.dense.count }

    has :: (&self, e: Entity) -> bool {
        match (self.index.get(e)) { some (_) { true }  none { false } }
    }

    // Returns a POINTER, not a value, and that is not a style choice.
    // `get` returning `Option<C>` gives you a copy, and a movement system that
    // writes the new position back has to hash the entity a second time to
    // store it. So the reader of a mutable container returns a place.
    //
    // FRICTION 16: `self.dense.items[i]` reaches past `[]` into List's raw
    // pointer field, because `[] :: (&self, i: u32) -> C` yields a value and
    // there is no way to spell "the slot". `xs[i] = v` cannot be written for
    // any user container in this language. json.z hid this: `List.push` writes
    // `self.items[...]` from INSIDE List, where reaching into the field is
    // legitimate. From outside it is a layering violation that the language
    // requires.
    at :: (&self, e: Entity) -> Option<ptr<C>> {
        match (self.index.get(e)) {
            none     { Option<ptr<C>>.none() }
            some (i) { Option<ptr<C>>.some(&self.dense.items[i]) }
        }
    }

    add :: (&self, e: Entity, c: C) -> () {
        match (self.index.get(e)) {
            some (i) { self.dense.items[i] = c }      // overwrite in place
            none     {
                self.index.put(e, self.dense.count)
                self.dense.push(c)
                self.owner.push(e)
            }
        }
    }

    // Swap-remove, so the dense array stays dense. The moved element's index
    // has to be repaired, which is the one place a sparse set is subtle.
    remove :: (&self, e: Entity) -> bool {
        i := match (self.index.get(e)) { some (i) { i }  none { return false } }
        last := self.dense.count - 1
        if (i != last) {
            self.dense.items[i] = self.dense.items[last]
            moved := self.owner.items[last]
            self.owner.items[i] = moved
            self.index.put(moved, i)
        }
        // Three results dropped on the floor: `pop` yields `Option<C>` and
        // `remove` a bool, and nothing in the language says whether ignoring a
        // return value is allowed, warned about, or an error. Small, but it is
        // a decision, and `try`/`Result` make it matter.
        self.dense.pop()
        self.owner.pop()
        self.index.remove(e)
        true
    }

    // Iteration is over the dense arrays, by index, because there is no
    // `for (x in xs)` form and building a ListIter per store per system costs
    // more syntax than the index loop it replaces. See FRICTION 18.
    entity_at :: (&self, i: u32) -> Entity  { self.owner.items[i] }
    slot_at   :: (&self, i: u32) -> ptr<C>  { &self.dense.items[i] }

    deinit :: (&self) -> () {
        self.index.deinit()
        self.owner.deinit()
        self.dense.deinit()
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// ecs.store  --  erasing it
// ============================================================================
//
// [W8] A GENERIC TYPE ERASES TO A NON-GENERIC SHAPE, as long as no hole in the
// shape mentions the type parameter. `Store<Vec2, M>` and `Store<Health, M>`
// are different types with different vtables, and both coerce to
// `ptr<AnyStore>` because `remove`, `count` and `deinit` never say `C`.
//
// That is NOTE 3's rule ("multiply the vtables, do not add defaults") read
// backwards, and it is what makes a heterogeneous component registry possible
// at all. It is the single largest thing that worked in this file.

AnyStore :: -> self {
    remove: (&self, e: Entity) -> bool
    count:  (&self) -> u32
    deinit: (&self) -> ()
}

// FRICTION 19, and it is a cost of structural matching that json.z never paid:
// `Store` satisfies `AnyStore` only because I NAMED its members `remove`,
// `count` and `deinit` to match. Had `Store` come from another library and
// called them `erase`, `len` and `dispose`, there would be no way to say "it
// satisfies AnyStore via this renaming" -- structural matching has no adapter.
// You write a wrapper block per mismatch, and a wrapper costs a pointer, a
// vtable and a name.
//
//     StoreAsAny :: <C, M> (inner: ptr<Store<C, M>>) -> self {
//         remove :: (&self, e: Entity) -> bool { self.inner.erase(e) }
//         count  :: (&self) -> u32 { self.inner.len() }
//         deinit :: (&self) -> ()  { self.inner.dispose() }
//     }
//
// Go has the same hole and lives with it. Worth writing down rather than
// discovering it in a user's bug report.


// ============================================================================
// game.components
// ============================================================================

Health :: (hp: i32, max: i32) -> self

// A state machine as a sum, with the state's data in the state. This is what
// `-> oneof` is for and it reads exactly right.
Ai :: -> oneof {
    idle:  ()
    hunt:  Entity            // who we are chasing
    flee:  Vec2              // where we are running to
    dead:  ()
}

// FRICTION 20, which json.z raised as FRICTION 7 and which is much worse here.
// "Is this entity dead" is a tag test with no payload to bind, and there is no
// way to say it. Every such question becomes a match with a tail of dead arms:
is_dead :: (a: Ai) -> bool {
    match (a) { dead { true }  idle { false }  hunt (_) { false }  flee (_) { false } }
}
// The ECS version of the same gap is worse than a bool: a real ECS keys its
// archetypes off a COMPONENT BITMASK, and a bitmask needs each variant to have
// an ordinal. A sum's discriminant is not a value, cannot be compared, cannot
// index an array, and cannot be serialised. See [P19].


// ============================================================================
// ecs.world
// ============================================================================
//
// FRICTION 21, the largest new result in the file: A `-> self` BLOCK HAS NO
// CONSTRUCTOR BODY, so a self-referential structure cannot be built.
//
// What the World wants is four typed stores AND a list of pointers to those
// same stores, so that despawn and teardown can sweep every component type
// without naming it. Written the obvious way:
//
//     World :: <M: Allocator> (mem: M) -> self {
//         pos    := Store<Vec2, M>(mem)
//         hp     := Store<Health, M>(mem)
//         stores := List<ptr<AnyStore>, M>(mem)
//         stores.push(&self.pos)          // <- no `self` here, and no
//         stores.push(&self.hp)           //    statement position either
//     }
//
// Two separate things stop it, and only the first is about syntax:
//
//   1. There is no statement position in a `-> self` block. A field
//      initialiser is an expression; there is nowhere to run a step that is
//      not a binding. json.z hit this as FRICTION 5 and read it as being about
//      FAILURE, and [P14] (`-> Result<self, E>`) was adopted for it. [P14] is
//      the wrong half of the fix: it gives the constructor a RETURN TYPE and
//      still no BODY. `try` in a field initialiser has nothing to unwind to.
//
//   2. `&self.pos` during construction is a pointer into a value that is about
//      to be returned to the caller. THE LANGUAGE HAS NO STATEMENT ABOUT
//      WHETHER VALUES MOVE. json.z never noticed because no interior pointer
//      it took outlived the expression that took it -- `&b` in `to_string`,
//      `&arena` in main, `&tally(&n)` as an argument. Every one of those is a
//      pointer to a local that stays put. The moment a structure points into
//      itself, "is a returned block copied or constructed in place" becomes a
//      question the language must answer, and it does not have the vocabulary
//      to ask it. See [P18].
//
// The workaround below is two-phase construction, which is exactly what the
// unified declaration form was supposed to abolish: a `wire` you must remember
// to call, and a World that is unsafe to use between the two lines.

World :: <M: Allocator> (mem: M) -> self {

    next_id := 1
    recycle := List<Entity, M>(mem)       // despawned ids, ready for reuse
    live    := Map<Entity, bool, M>(mem)

    pos   := Store<Vec2, M>(mem)
    vel   := Store<Vec2, M>(mem)
    hp    := Store<Health, M>(mem)
    brain := Store<Ai, M>(mem)

    // Declared here, defined two hundred lines below. json.z's FRICTION 10
    // (order independence is required and unstated) is load-bearing again.
    died  := Signal<Entity, M>(mem)

    // Bound here and POPULATED by `wire`, below, because populating it needs
    // `self` and there is nowhere in this block that `self` exists.
    stores := List<ptr<AnyStore>, M>(mem)
    wired  := false

    // PHASE TWO. Everything that needs `self` lives here, and the type is
    // unusable until it has run.
    wire :: (&self) -> () {
        if (self.wired) { return }
        self.stores.push(&self.pos)
        self.stores.push(&self.vel)
        self.stores.push(&self.hp)
        self.stores.push(&self.brain)
        self.wired = true
    }

    spawn :: (&self) -> Entity {
        e := match (self.recycle.pop()) {
            some (old) { Entity(old.id, old.gen + 1) }
            none       { id := self.next_id; self.next_id += 1; Entity(id, 1) }
        }
        self.live.put(e, true)
        e
    }

    // The erased sweep, and the reason `stores` exists: despawn must remove
    // this entity from EVERY component type, and it must not know which types
    // those are. Three of the five items in this file's header meet here.
    despawn :: (&self, e: Entity) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < self.stores.count; i += 1) {
            self.stores[i].remove(e)             // indirect call, one per type
        }
        self.live.remove(e)
        self.recycle.push(e)
    }

    alive :: (&self, e: Entity) -> bool {
        match (self.live.get(e)) { some (b) { b }  none { false } }
    }

    deinit :: (&self) -> () {
        // By hand, in reverse declaration order, because `deinit` is a
        // naming convention and nothing calls it for you -- FRICTION 22.
        // NOT through `stores` either: that would be four indirect calls to
        // reach four fields that are statically right here. Erasure earns its
        // keep for `despawn`, which is per-entity and dynamic, and not for
        // teardown, which is per-type and static.
        self.brain.deinit()
        self.hp.deinit()
        self.vel.deinit()
        self.pos.deinit()
        self.stores.deinit()
        self.died.deinit()
        self.live.deinit()
        self.recycle.deinit()
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// ecs.query  --  joining two stores
// ============================================================================
//
// FRICTION 23. The natural signature for a join is
//
//     next :: (&self) -> Option<{e: Entity, a: ptr<A>, b: ptr<B>}>
//
// -- json.z's [W4], the partial projection, used as a RESULT TYPE. It does not
// work, and the reason is worth stating: `-> {a, b}` names no type. It is a
// projection, which is a property of a DECLARATION, and `Option<T>` needs a
// TYPE. So the multiple-return form, which json.z found to be the thing you
// reach for constantly, does not compose with generics: the moment a
// multiple-return has to be stored, wrapped in an Option, or put in a list, it
// must be given a name and become an ordinary record.
//
// Naming it brings the plumbing parameters back with it, which is json.z's
// FRICTION 1 arriving at a second, unrelated site.

Hit :: <A, B> (e: Entity, a: ptr<A>, b: ptr<B>) -> self

Join2 :: <A, B, M> (xs: ptr<Store<A, M>>, ys: ptr<Store<B, M>>) -> self {

    i := 0

    // Iterate the smaller store and probe the larger -- the standard join, and
    // the reason `count` is on the store at all.
    next :: (&self) -> Option<Hit<A, B>> {
        while (self.i < self.xs.count()) {
            e := self.xs.entity_at(self.i)
            a := self.xs.slot_at(self.i)
            self.i += 1
            match (self.ys.at(e)) {
                some (b) { return Option<Hit<A, B>>.some(Hit(e, a, b)) }
                none     { }
            }
        }
        Option<Hit<A, B>>.none()
    }
}

// FRICTION 24. `Join2`, `Join3`, `Join4`. json.z's FRICTION 6 called the
// `Fn`/`Fn2` family "cheap enough to live with". At a query API it is not
// cosmetic any more: a system that reads three components and writes a fourth
// is the ordinary case, and the arity family is now part of the surface a user
// of the library has to learn.

join2 :: <A, B, M> (xs: ptr<Store<A, M>>, ys: ptr<Store<B, M>>) -> Join2<A, B, M> {
    Join2(xs, ys)
}


// ============================================================================
// ecs.system
// ============================================================================
//
// A shape, so systems can be held in one list and run in order. `M` is here
// only because `World` has it, which is json.z's FRICTION 2 (monomorphisation
// is viral through containing types) reappearing in a SHAPE rather than in
// data. The alias trick that fixed it for `Json` does not apply: a library
// cannot name the application's allocator, so `System` stays generic and every
// implementer of it does too.

System :: <M: Allocator> -> self {
    run:  (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, dt: f32) -> ()
    name: (&self) -> slice<u8>
}

// A stateless system is a block with no fields, so it is zero-sized, and the
// `ptr<System<M>>` in the schedule is one word plus a shared vtable.
Movement :: <M: Allocator> () -> self {
    name :: (&self) -> slice<u8> { "movement" }
    run :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, dt: f32) -> () {
        it := join2(&w.pos, &w.vel)
        loop {
            h := match (it.next()) { some (h) { h }  none { return } }
            // Mutation in place, through the pointer the join yielded.
            // `h.a^ = ...` -- the pointee is written, and nothing in the type
            // says `run` may do that while `name` may not. FRICTION 25.
            h.a^ = Num<Vec2>.add(h.a^, Scale<Vec2, f32>.scale(h.b^, dt))
        }
    }
}

// A system WITH state: the tuning constant is a field, so it is a runtime
// param of the block, which is json.z's [W2] again -- params of a `-> self`
// def are the fields, and a configured system needs no new form.
Decay :: <M: Allocator> (rate: f32) -> self {
    name :: (&self) -> slice<u8> { "decay" }
    run :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, dt: f32) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < w.vel.count(); i += 1) {
            v := w.vel.slot_at(i)
            v^ = Scale<Vec2, f32>.scale(v^, 1.0 - self.rate * dt)
        }
    }
}

Reaper :: <M: Allocator> (doomed: ptr<List<Entity, M>>) -> self {
    name :: (&self) -> slice<u8> { "reaper" }
    run :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, dt: f32) -> () {
        // Collect first, despawn second: `despawn` swap-removes inside every
        // store, so mutating while iterating a dense array is the same bug it
        // is in every other language. Nothing in the type system helps.
        self.doomed.count = 0
        for (i := 0; i < w.hp.count(); i += 1) {
            h := w.hp.slot_at(i)
            if (h.hp <= 0) { self.doomed.push(w.hp.entity_at(i)) }
        }
        for (i := 0; i < self.doomed.count; i += 1) {
            e := self.doomed.items[i]
            w.died.emit(e)                     // see Signal, below
            w.despawn(e)
        }
    }
}

// The schedule. Every element is a fat pointer, and NOTHING OWNS THE POINTEES:
// the concrete `Movement`, `Decay` and `Reaper` blocks have to be locals in
// main that outlive the schedule, because there is no way to store a shape BY
// VALUE and no box to put one in. See FRICTION 26 and [P20].
Schedule :: <M: Allocator> (mem: M) -> self {
    items := List<ptr<System<M>>, M>(mem)

    add :: (&self, s: ptr<System<M>>) -> () { self.items.push(s) }

    tick :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, dt: f32) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < self.items.count; i += 1) { self.items[i].run(w, dt) }
    }

    deinit :: (&self) -> () { self.items.deinit() }
}


// ============================================================================
// ecs.signal  --  callables stored in data
// ============================================================================
//
// json.z passed one closure to one function and it worked ([P15]: a closure is
// a block with a `()` member). KEEPING one is a different question, and it is
// the third thing this file was written to find out.

Signal :: <E, M: Allocator> (mem: M) -> self {

    subs := List<ptr<Fn<E, ()>>, M>(mem)

    // FRICTION 26 concretely. The argument is a fat pointer, so the CALLER
    // owns the closure block and must keep it alive for as long as the signal
    // lives. In main below, the subscriber is a local declared before the
    // world and never mentioned again -- if it went out of scope first, this
    // list would hold two dangling words and nothing would say so.
    on :: (&self, f: ptr<Fn<E, ()>>) -> () { self.subs.push(f) }

    // FRICTION 27. There is no `off`. Unsubscribing means finding the
    // subscriber again, and a `ptr<Fn<E,()>>` is a PAIR of words built at the
    // coercion site. Two coercions of the same block are two fat pointers; are
    // they equal? unified.z says a fat pointer is "built at the moment of
    // coercion, and only there", which suggests yes for the data half and
    // nothing at all about the vtable half. Identity of an erased value is
    // undefined, so an observer list can be added to and never pruned.
    emit :: (&self, e: E) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < self.subs.count; i += 1) {
            self.subs[i](e)                   // -> self.subs[i].()(e)  [P15]
        }
    }

    deinit :: (&self) -> () { self.subs.deinit() }
}

// A subscriber that has to mutate the thing that is emitting to it. This is
// the ordinary case for an event bus and it is where FRICTION 25 stops being
// theoretical: `w` is a `ptr<World<M>>` captured by a closure that `World`
// itself calls, from inside `Reaper.run`, while `Reaper.run` is iterating
// `w.hp`. Nothing in any of the three signatures says who may write what, so
// nothing can flag the reentrancy.
on_death :: <M: Allocator> (log: ptr<List<Entity, M>>) (e: Entity) -> () {
    log.push(e)
}


// ============================================================================
// game.combat  --  a system that reads a sum and writes two components
// ============================================================================

Combat :: <M: Allocator> (bite: i32) -> self {

    name :: (&self) -> slice<u8> { "combat" }

    run :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, dt: f32) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < w.brain.count(); i += 1) {
            e := w.brain.entity_at(i)
            a := w.brain.slot_at(i)

            // A state machine step. The match is an EXPRESSION, so the whole
            // transition is one assignment -- json.z's best structural result,
            // and it holds here.
            a^ = match (a^) {
                idle       { self.wake(w, e) }
                hunt (t)   { self.chase(w, e, t) }
                flee (to)  { self.run_away(w, e, to) }
                dead       { Ai.dead() }
            }
        }
    }

    wake :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, e: Entity) -> Ai {
        match (self.nearest(w, e)) {
            some (t) { Ai.hunt(t) }
            none     { Ai.idle() }
        }
    }

    chase :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, e: Entity, t: Entity) -> Ai {
        if (!w.alive(t)) { return Ai.idle() }

        // Four pointers out of two stores, each an Option, each needing an
        // arm. `match ... { some (x) { x }  _ { return } }` per line is
        // json.z's binding idiom and it is still the right shape -- but see
        // FRICTION 28: every one of these is a hash lookup that the join
        // above already did, and there is no way to hold onto the result.
        me   := match (w.pos.at(e)) { some (p) { p }  none { return Ai.idle() } }
        prey := match (w.pos.at(t)) { some (p) { p }  none { return Ai.idle() } }

        d := Num<Vec2>.sub(prey^, me^)
        if (length_sq(d) < 1.0) {
            match (w.hp.at(t)) {
                some (h) { h.hp -= self.bite }
                none     { }
            }
            return Ai.hunt(t)
        }

        match (w.vel.at(e)) {
            some (v) { v^ = Scale<Vec2, f32>.scale(d, 0.5) }
            none     { }
        }
        Ai.hunt(t)
    }

    run_away :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, e: Entity, to: Vec2) -> Ai {
        me := match (w.pos.at(e)) { some (p) { p }  none { return Ai.idle() } }
        if (length_sq(Num<Vec2>.sub(to, me^)) < 1.0) { return Ai.idle() }
        Ai.flee(to)
    }

    nearest :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, e: Entity) -> Option<Entity> {
        me   := match (w.pos.at(e)) { some (p) { p }  none { return Option<Entity>.none() } }
        best := Option<Entity>.none()
        bd   := 1000000.0
        for (i := 0; i < w.hp.count(); i += 1) {
            t := w.hp.entity_at(i)
            if (same(t, e)) { continue }
            match (w.pos.at(t)) {
                some (p) {
                    d := length_sq(Num<Vec2>.sub(p^, me^))
                    if (d < bd) { bd = d; best = Option<Entity>.some(t) }
                }
                none { }
            }
        }
        best
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// game.print
// ============================================================================

:: -> fmt.Formattable<Vec2> {
    format = (v, w) -> () {
        w.write("(")
        fmt.Formattable<f32>.format(v.x, w)
        w.write(", ")
        fmt.Formattable<f32>.format(v.y, w)
        w.write(")")
    }
}

// A PARAMETERISED, NAMED policy instance -- the case NOTE 7 argues from and
// which nothing in json.z actually exercised. Three things show up at once:
//
//   - `indent: u32 = 2` is a COMPILE-TIME VALUE parameter. unified.z says
//     compile-time args come in two kinds, inferred-from-a-runtime-arg and
//     solved-by-search. A value parameter is a third kind and is never
//     mentioned, though `capacity :: 10` in `List` implies it. FRICTION 29.
//
//   - `M` must be inferred and `indent` must be given, so the caller cannot
//     pass positionally and must write `dump<indent = 4>`. Named compile-time
//     arguments stop being a convenience and become the only way to call this.
//
//   - `self.row(...)` is [P16] carrying `indent` down with it. Written
//     `dump.row(...)` the nested levels would silently reset to 2, and written
//     `Formattable<World<M>>.row(...)` it would not compile, since `row` is a
//     fresh member and not one of the type's holes. The distinction NOTE 7
//     introduced is doing exactly the work it was introduced for.
dump :: <M, indent: u32 = 2> -> fmt.Formattable<World<M>> {

    format = (v, w) -> () {
        w.write("world {\n")
        for (i := 0; i < v.pos.count(); i += 1) {
            e := v.pos.entity_at(i)
            self.row(v, e, w)
        }
        w.write("}\n")
    }

    // Creates a member; not one of Formattable's holes, so `::` and not `=`.
    // The glossary calls this out and it turned out to be the common case:
    // this instance has one hole and two helpers.
    row :: (v: World<M>, e: Entity, w: ptr<Writer>) -> () {
        self.pad(w)
        w.write("#")
        fmt.Formattable<u32>.format(e.id, w)
        w.write(" ")
        match (v.pos.at(e)) { some (p) { fmt.Formattable<Vec2>.format(p^, w) }  none { } }
        match (v.hp.at(e))  { some (h) { w.write(" hp="); fmt.Formattable<i32>.format(h.hp, w) }  none { } }
        match (v.brain.at(e)) {
            some (a) {
                w.write(" ")
                w.write(match (a^) {
                    idle     { "idle" }
                    hunt (_) { "hunt" }
                    flee (_) { "flee" }
                    dead     { "dead" }
                })
            }
            none { }
        }
        w.write("\n")
    }

    pad :: (w: ptr<Writer>) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < indent; i += 1) { w.write(" ") }
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// main
// ============================================================================

main :: () -> () {
    heap  := mem.Heap()
    arena := mem.Arena(page, 1 << 20)

    // json.z's alias trick, applied to a second type. It works exactly as well
    // here, and it is worth noting that this line is the ONLY place in the
    // program below `World` that an allocator is named.
    A :: ptr<Arena>

    w := World<A>(&arena)
    w.wire()                                // <- FRICTION 21. Forget it and
    defer w.deinit()                        //    `despawn` silently does
                                            //    nothing at all.

    // THE OWNERSHIP DANCE. Every one of these is a local whose ADDRESS goes
    // into a list that outlives this statement, so declaration order here is
    // load-bearing and nothing in the language enforces it. Written in the
    // other order it compiles and dangles. See FRICTION 26.
    move   := Movement<A>()
    decay  := Decay<A>(0.1)
    fight  := Combat<A>(3)
    doomed := List<Entity, A>(&arena)
    reap   := Reaper<A>(&doomed)

    sched := Schedule<A>(&arena)
    defer sched.deinit()
    sched.add(&move)                        // &Movement<A> -> ptr<System<A>>
    sched.add(&decay)
    sched.add(&fight)
    sched.add(&reap)

    // A stored closure. `obituary` is a block with one field (`&log`) and one
    // `()` member; `&obituary` is the coercion to `ptr<Fn<Entity, ()>>`, built
    // here and only here, exactly as `boxed : ptr<Allocator>` is in unified.z.
    log      := List<Entity, A>(&arena)
    obituary := on_death<A>(&log)
    w.died.on(&obituary)

    // Spawn: three predators and one very unlucky herbivore.
    for (i := 0; i < 3; i += 1) {
        e := w.spawn()
        // `f32_of` is invented. Neither file has a story for numeric
        // conversion, and under [P17] it is one more policy (`From<A, B>`).
        w.pos.add(e, Vec2(f32_of(i), 0.0))
        w.vel.add(e, Vec2(1.0, 0.0))
        w.hp.add(e, Health(10, 10))
        w.brain.add(e, Ai.idle())
    }

    prey := w.spawn()
    w.pos.add(prey, Vec2(0.5, 0.0))
    w.hp.add(prey, Health(4, 4))
    // No Velocity and no Ai: the join in `Movement` skips it, which is the
    // whole point of a component system and the one part of this program that
    // needed no comment while writing it.

    for (t := 0; t < 4; t += 1) { sched.tick(&w, 0.5) }

    io.out.write(fmt.to_string(heap, w))                  // pooled: no instance
    io.out.write(fmt.to_string<F = dump<indent = 4>>(heap, w))

    // `dump` is NAMED, so it is never searched and the first line above has no
    // instance to find. json.z's `spaced` had the same property and hid it,
    // because `Formattable<Json>` also had an anonymous instance in the pool.
    // Here there is none, so the first line is an error and the file has to
    // choose: publish `dump<>` anonymously, or make every caller name it.
    // FRICTION 30 -- there is no way to say "this instance, and also pooled".

    io.out.write("died: ")
    io.out.write(fmt.to_string(heap, log.count))
}


// ============================================================================
// ============================================================================
//
//                                F I N D I N G S
//
// ============================================================================
// ============================================================================
//
// Numbering continues json.z: [W8+], FRICTION 13+, [P17+].
//
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT HELD UP
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// [W8]  A GENERIC TYPE ERASES TO A NON-GENERIC SHAPE when no hole in the shape
//       mentions the parameter. `Store<Vec2,M>` and `Store<Health,M>` are
//       different types with different vtables and both are `ptr<AnyStore>`.
//       This is NOTE 3's rule read backwards and it is what makes a
//       heterogeneous registry possible at all. Without it `despawn` would
//       have to name every component type in the application.
//
// [W9]  "PARAMS OF A `-> self` DEF ARE THE FIELDS" survives its second file
//       and is still the highest-value line in the language. A configured
//       system -- `Decay(0.1)`, `Combat(3)`, `Reaper(&doomed)` -- needs no
//       builder, no config struct and no new form, and a stateless one is
//       `Movement()`: no fields, zero bytes, one shared vtable behind
//       `ptr<System<M>>`.
//
// [W10] MATCH-AS-EXPRESSION IS WHAT MAKES SUM-TYPED STATE MACHINES READABLE.
//       The entire AI transition is one assignment:
//
//           a^ = match (a^) { idle {...} hunt (t) {...} flee (to) {...} dead {...} }
//
//       Every arm has to produce the same type, so a forgotten transition is a
//       type error rather than a state that quietly never changes. This is the
//       strongest argument in either file for `match` being an expression.
//
// [W11] THE SHAPE/POLICY TEST ANSWERS QUESTIONS IT WAS NOT DESIGNED FOR.
//       "Can there legitimately be two?" applied to operators says `[]` and
//       `()` are shapes and `+` is a policy, which is exactly the split the
//       implementation needs, and it was derived rather than decided. When a
//       one-line test keeps producing the right answer in cases nobody had in
//       mind, that is evidence the abstraction is real.
//
// [W12] [P16] PAYS OFF EXACTLY WHERE NOTE 7 SAID IT WOULD. `dump<indent>` is
//       the parameterised named instance NOTE 7 argues from, and json.z never
//       actually had one. `self.row(...)` carries `indent` down; `dump.row(...)`
//       would reset every nested level to the default; and
//       `Formattable<World<M>>.row(...)` does not even typecheck, because
//       `row` is a fresh member and not one of the type's holes. Three
//       spellings, three distinct meanings, all visible in the text.
//
// [W13] THE ALIAS TRICK GENERALISES. `A :: ptr<Arena>` in main is the only
//       mention of an allocator in the whole application layer, exactly as
//       `Doc :: Json<ptr<Arena>>` was in json.z. Two unrelated programs, same
//       one-line fix, no library changes: this is now a pattern rather than a
//       coincidence and belongs in the language's documentation.
//
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHERE IT FOUGHT BACK
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Ordered by how much they cost, not by discovery.
//
// FRICTION 25 -- NOTHING SAYS WHO MAY WRITE WHAT, and in a simulation this
//   stops being a documentation problem. json.z raised it as FRICTION 8 with
//   `get` and `put` both taking `&self`. Here:
//
//     - `Store.at` hands out a `ptr<C>` into a `List` that reallocates on
//       `push`. The pointer is valid until anyone adds a component of that
//       type. Nothing in the signature, the shape or the call site says so.
//     - `Reaper.run` iterates `w.hp` and calls `w.died.emit`, which calls a
//       closure holding `ptr<World<M>>`, which may despawn -- which
//       swap-removes inside `w.hp`, under the loop. Three signatures, no
//       reentrancy anywhere in them.
//     - `Combat.chase` holds `me` and `prey`, two interior pointers into the
//       same store, and writes through one of them.
//
//   This is now the file's top item. `::`/`:=` encode immutable/mutable for
//   BINDINGS and there is still no counterpart for POINTEES, so a `ptr<T>` is
//   a fully mutable, unrestricted, indefinitely-lived handle. Note the shape
//   of the fix matters more than usual: the language's whole story is that
//   dispatch and cost are visible in the type, and aliasing is the one cost
//   that currently is not.
//
// FRICTION 21 -- A `-> self` BLOCK HAS NO CONSTRUCTOR BODY, so a structure
//   cannot refer to itself. See the long note above `World`. json.z met the
//   same wall (FRICTION 5) and read it as being about FAILURE; [P14]
//   (`-> Result<self, E>`) was adopted and gives the constructor a RETURN TYPE
//   while still giving it no BODY. `try` in a field initialiser has nothing to
//   unwind to and no place to run.
//
//   The cost here is two-phase construction -- `w.wire()` -- which is the
//   pattern the unified form was meant to abolish, and which is unchecked:
//   forget the line and `despawn` sweeps an empty list forever.  -> [P21]
//
// FRICTION 13 -- NO OPERATOR STORY BEYOND `[]` AND `()`.  RESOLVED -> [P17] `2.0 * v` cannot be
//   written: the member would have to belong to f32, UFCS would resolve a free
//   `*` in the caller's scope, and NOTE 4 forbids a free function from
//   satisfying a shape -- correctly, since otherwise "is Vec2 numeric" would
//   depend on imports. Without a fix, user types get `+` and not `*` by a
//   scalar, and no bound can require arithmetic of a type parameter.  -> [P17]
//
// FRICTION 16 -- `[]` CANNOT BE ASSIGNED THROUGH. `[] :: (&self, i: u32) -> T`
//   yields a value, so `xs[i] = v` is unwritable for every user container in
//   the language. Every mutation in this file goes through `.items[i]` or a
//   `ptr<C>` from `at`. json.z hid this because `List.push` writes
//   `self.items[...]` from INSIDE List, where the raw field is in scope.
//   The language has no PLACE concept: no way for a member to name a slot
//   rather than produce a copy.  -> [P22]
//
// FRICTION 17 -- SO A CONTAINER CANNOT BE ENCAPSULATED. This is FRICTION 16's
//   consequence and it is worse than it, because it invalidates a feature
//   already adopted. json.z's [P5] says the projection is the export list:
//
//       List :: <T, M> (mem: M) -> {push, pop, count, []} { ... }
//
//   Do that and this file stops compiling -- `self.dense.items[i] = c`,
//   `self.doomed.count = 0`, and json.z's own JSON printer, which reaches into
//   `m.slots` and `m.cap` because `Map` has no iterator. Today every container
//   in both files must export its raw storage, and [P5] is unusable on exactly
//   the types it was meant for. Places ([P22]) and iteration ([P23]) are what
//   make visibility affordable.
//
// FRICTION 26 -- AN ERASED VALUE IS A POINTER, AND NOTHING OWNS THE POINTEE.
//   `List<ptr<System<M>>>` holds four fat pointers into four locals in main,
//   and the schedule outlives the statement that made each one. Declaration
//   order in main is load-bearing and unenforced; reorder two lines and it
//   compiles and dangles. There is no way to store a shape BY VALUE (the size
//   is not known) and no box to put one in.
//
//   This is the price of "erasure is a coercion at one line" ([W7] in json.z),
//   which is otherwise the design's best trick, and it is only visible once
//   erased values are STORED rather than passed.  -> [P20]
//
// FRICTION 22 -- TEARDOWN IS A NAMING CONVENTION. `deinit` is an ordinary
//   member that happens to be spelled the same everywhere. Nothing calls it,
//   `defer` is lexical so it only helps for locals, and `List<T>` does not
//   destroy its elements -- so `Store<Store<...>, M>` leaks and no diagnostic
//   exists. `World.deinit` names its four stores explicitly, in reverse
//   declaration order, by hand.
//
//   Note the erased list does NOT help: sweeping `stores` for teardown would
//   be four indirect calls to reach four fields that are statically right
//   there. Erasure earns its keep for `despawn`, which is per-entity and
//   dynamic, and not for teardown, which is per-type and static.
//
// FRICTION 14 -- A POLICY HAS ONLY INPUT PARAMETERS.  RESOLVED -> [P24] `Scale<V, S>` wants to
//   say "and the result type is whatever V's scaling produces". It cannot, so
//   the result is pinned to V and any operation whose output type differs from
//   its inputs needs a third parameter that the caller must supply and the
//   solver cannot infer.
//
//   The same gap is already visible in json.z and was not recognised there:
//
//       Iterator :: <T> -> self { next: (&self) -> Option<T> }
//       fold :: <T, A, I: Iterator<T>, F: Fn2<A, T, A>> (it: ptr<I>, ...) -> A
//
//   `T` is a property OF the iterator, not a choice the caller makes, and it
//   is threaded through `fold`'s signature only because the shape has nowhere
//   to put it. A shape needs to be able to declare a type its implementer
//   supplies.  -> [P24], which turns out to cost nothing.
//
// FRICTION 20 -- A SUM'S DISCRIMINANT IS NOT A VALUE. json.z's FRICTION 7
//   wanted `expect(Token.colon())` to mean "same variant, ignore payload".
//   Here the same gap blocks three separate things: a tag test (`is_dead`
//   spells out four arms to compute a bool), an archetype BITMASK (the actual
//   data structure a real ECS uses, which needs one bit per component type and
//   therefore an ordinal per variant), and serialisation (a saved game must
//   write a stable number for `Ai.hunt`). "Ignore the payload" was the small
//   version of this.  -> [P19]
//
// FRICTION 23 -- A PARTIAL PROJECTION IS NOT A TYPE. json.z's [W4] found
//   `-> {at, found}` to be the thing you reach for constantly. Its limit is
//   that `-> {a, b}` is a property of a DECLARATION and names nothing, so
//   `Option<{e: Entity, a: ptr<A>}>` cannot be written and every join result
//   has to become a named record (`Hit<A, B>`) -- which brings the plumbing
//   parameters with it. The multiple-return form does not compose with
//   generics, and that is precisely where container code needs it.
//
// FRICTION 24 -- ARITY FAMILIES REACH THE PUBLIC SURFACE. json.z called
//   `Fn`/`Fn2` "cheap enough to live with". `Join2`, `Join3`, `Join4` are not
//   an implementation detail; they are what a user of the ECS types every day,
//   and a system reading three components and writing a fourth is the ordinary
//   case, not the exotic one. Variadic compile-time args are the only real
//   answer and nothing cheaper was found.
//
// FRICTION 18 -- THERE IS NO `for (x in xs)`, so every traversal in this file
//   is a C index loop and `Iterator<T>` -- a shape both files declare -- is
//   used exactly once, by `fold`, in json.z. A `loop` + `match` + `Option`
//   consumer is four lines to replace one, so nobody writes it, so the
//   abstraction that the standard library is built around goes unused in the
//   two programs written against it. That is worth taking as a signal.  -> [P23]
//
// FRICTION 28 -- POINTERS INTO A CONTAINER ARE THE ONLY MUTABLE HANDLE, and
//   the join hands out two per hit. `Combat.chase` then re-looks-up the same
//   entity in the same store because there is no way to keep the pointer the
//   join already found across a call that might have invalidated it. Half a
//   performance problem and half FRICTION 25.
//
// FRICTION 19 -- STRUCTURAL MATCHING HAS NO ADAPTER. `Store` satisfies
//   `AnyStore` only because I chose the member names to match. A foreign type
//   with the right operations under different names cannot be adapted without
//   a wrapper block costing a pointer, a vtable and a name. Go has the same
//   hole; worth writing down rather than rediscovering.
//
// FRICTION 29 -- COMPILE-TIME VALUE PARAMETERS ARE UNSTATED.  RESOLVED: they
//   are now the third kind of compile-time argument in unified.z. `dump<indent: u32
//   = 2>` is a compile-time parameter whose type is not a type. unified.z says
//   compile-time args come in TWO kinds, inferred-from-a-runtime-argument and
//   solved-by-search; this is a third, it is used by `List`'s `capacity :: 10`
//   and by every array size, and the file never mentions it. It also forces
//   named passing: `M` is inferred and `indent` is not, so `dump<indent = 4>`
//   is the only spelling available.
//
// FRICTION 15 -- NO EQUALITY ON USER TYPES.  RESOLVED -> [P17], `Eq<A, B>` `Key<Entity>.equals(a, b)` is what
//   an `==` costs today, and `Key` exists to pair hashing with equality for a
//   map -- using it as the equality mechanism is an accident. Subsumed by
//   [P17], which puts `==` in an `Eq<T>` policy where `Ord`, `Num` and `Key`
//   already live.
//
// FRICTION 27 -- THE IDENTITY OF AN ERASED VALUE IS UNDEFINED, so `Signal` has
//   no `off`. A `ptr<Shape>` is two words built at the coercion site; whether
//   two coercions of one block compare equal is not stated, and the vtable
//   half is a compiler-chosen address. Any API that removes a registered
//   callback needs an answer -- probably a handle returned by `on`, but the
//   language-level question (is `ptr<Shape>` comparable) still has to be
//   settled.
//
// FRICTION 30 -- AN INSTANCE IS EITHER POOLED OR NAMED, NEVER BOTH. Naming is
//   what removes it from the pool, so `dump` cannot also be the default
//   `Formattable<World<M>>` and `fmt.to_string(heap, w)` in main has nothing
//   to find. json.z hid this: `spaced` was a named SECOND instance and an
//   anonymous first one already existed.
//
//   RESOLVED BY IDIOM, no proposal needed -- publish a forwarder:
//
//       :: <M> -> fmt.Formattable<World<M>> { format = (v, w) -> () { dump<>.format(v, w) } }
//
//   It costs one line, it makes "the default is `dump` with indent 2" explicit
//   rather than implicit, and it is exactly what `reverse_u8` does in reverse.
//   Document it; do not add a keyword.
//
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PROPOSALS
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// STATUS. [P17] and [P24] are ADOPTED and now live in unified.z, together with
// [P25] and [P26] below, which came out of adopting them. They are kept here
// because the argument for each is the evidence, and the evidence is this file.
// [P18] [P19] [P20] [P21] [P22] [P23] are OPEN; five of the six are faces of
// the memory question and are now unified.z's single largest open item.
//
// Adopting [P17] and [P24] resolved FRICTION 13, 14, 15 and 29, and forced the
// coherence decision that had been open since before json.z.
//
// [P17] ARITHMETIC AND COMPARISON OPERATORS ARE POLICIES; `[]` AND `()` STAY
//       SHAPES. `a + b` is `Num<A>.add(a, b)`, `a == b` is `Eq<A>.eq(a, b)`,
//       `a < b` is `Ord<A>.compare(a, b) < 0`, all solver-found. `a[i]` and
//       `a(x)` stay members, because they take `self`.
//
//       Derived from unified.z's own test rather than chosen: two ways to add
//       two Vec2 (saturating, wrapping) -- yes, so it is a policy; two ways
//       for a List to index itself -- no, so it is a shape. Everything
//       awkward dissolves at once:
//
//         - `2.0 * v` works, because a policy is not attached to a type and
//           nobody has to own f32;
//         - primitives stop being special: `Num<f32>` is a pooled instance
//           like any other, and the language has no built-in arithmetic;
//         - NOTE 4 is untouched, because these are instances, not extrinsics;
//         - `lerp :: <T, N: Num<T> = ?>` -- a generic over "anything numeric"
//           -- becomes sayable, which it currently is not in any form;
//         - `Ord` and `Key` were already policies, so `<` and `==` land where
//           the standard library had already put them.
//
//       The cost is a fixed precedence table in the language, since a member
//       name carries none. That is unavoidable in any design and is not
//       user-extensible, which is the right call.
//
//       This is the highest value-per-word change available and it removes
//       machinery rather than adding it.
//
// [P21] THE BLOCK IS THE CONSTRUCTOR. A `-> self` block may contain plain
//       statements, run in textual order at construction, interleaved with the
//       `:=` field bindings. `self` becomes available at the point every `:=`
//       field is bound; before that, naming it is an error. With [P14]'s
//       `-> Result<self, E>` in the projection, `try` in that body means what
//       it means everywhere else.
//
//           World :: <M: Allocator> (mem: M) -> Result<self, E> {
//               pos    := Store<Vec2, M>(mem)
//               vel    := Store<Vec2, M>(mem)
//               stores := try List<ptr<AnyStore>, M>(mem)
//               stores.push(&self.pos)          // self is available: all
//               stores.push(&self.vel)          // fields are bound
//           }
//
//       This is not a new form -- a field initialiser is already a statement
//       that runs at construction; the proposal is that it need not be a
//       binding. It closes json.z's FRICTION 5, this file's FRICTION 21, and
//       makes [P14] mean something. It depends on [P18].
//
// [P18] SAY WHETHER VALUES MOVE. `stores.push(&self.pos)` takes the address of
//       a field of a block that is about to be handed to the caller. Whether
//       that pointer survives is currently unanswerable, because neither file
//       says whether returning a block copies it or constructs it in place.
//       json.z never noticed: every interior pointer it took (`&b`, `&arena`,
//       `&tally(&n)`) pointed at a local that stayed put.
//
//       The cheapest answer consistent with everything else here is: a value
//       has one address for its lifetime; `x := Foo()` constructs in place;
//       assigning a whole struct to an existing binding is a copy and it is
//       the programmer's business whether that invalidates interior pointers.
//       Whatever the answer, it must be WRITTEN DOWN -- self-referential
//       structures, closures stored in data, and `ptr<Shape>` in a container
//       all depend on it, and all three appear in the first non-trivial
//       program that stores anything.
//
// [P24] AN ASSOCIATED TYPE IS A HOLE WHOSE TYPE IS `type`. This costs nothing:
//       a hole is already "declared, not supplied", and a block may already
//       hold a compile-time type member (`A :: ptr<Arena>` in main is one).
//
//           Iterator :: -> self {
//               Item: type
//               next: (&self) -> Option<self.Item>
//           }
//
//           ListIter :: <T, M> (xs: ptr<List<T, M>>) -> self {
//               Item :: T                                  // fills the hole
//               next :: (&self) -> Option<T> { ... }
//           }
//
//       `fold :: <I: Iterator, A, F: Fn2<A, I.Item, A>> (it: ptr<I>, ...)`
//       loses a parameter, and the parameter it loses is the one the caller
//       never chose. Same for `Scale<V, S> { Out: type }`.
//
//       ONE AMENDMENT IS REQUIRED, and it is small: json.z's [P12] says a
//       block whose holes all take `self` is a shape and one whose holes take
//       none is a policy, and mixing is an error. A `: type` hole takes no
//       self and would make every shape carrying one an error. The test must
//       quantify over VALUE holes only; type holes are neither, in either kind
//       of block.
//
// [P22] PLACES. A member may yield a slot rather than a copy, so that
//       `xs[i] = v` is writable and containers can stop exporting their
//       storage. Two spellings were considered:
//
//         (a) `[] :: (&self, i: u32) -> ptr<T>`, with `xs[i]` meaning
//             `xs.[](i)^` in both value and assignment position. One rule, no
//             new concept, and the same trick works for a field accessor.
//         (b) a second member `[]=`, which is C++'s split and reintroduces
//             the paired-name problem [P15] removed.
//
//       (a). It also gives `Store.at` a better signature, and it is what makes
//       [P5]'s visibility usable on a container at all (FRICTION 17).
//
// [P23] `for (x := it) { ... }` over anything satisfying `Iterator`,
//       desugaring to the `loop` + `match` + `Option` consumer written by hand
//       four times in this file. With [P24], `for (h := join2(&w.pos, &w.vel))`
//       is the whole of a system's traversal. The evidence that this is not
//       sugar-for-its-own-sake: `Iterator<T>` is declared by both files and
//       used by neither, because the manual consumer is four times the size of
//       the index loop it replaces.
//
// [P19] A SUM'S DISCRIMINANT IS A VALUE. For `S :: -> oneof {...}`, `S.tag` is
//       a derived sum of unit variants, `tag_of(v): S.tag` is total, and it is
//       comparable, ordinal, and usable as an array index or a bit position.
//       `expect(Token.colon())` becomes `tag_of(t) == Token.tag.colon`, an
//       archetype mask becomes `1 << ordinal_of(tag)`, and a save file has a
//       stable number to write. Closes json.z's FRICTION 7 and this file's
//       FRICTION 20.
//
// [P20] OWNED ERASURE. `ptr<Shape>` is a borrow with no owner, which is right
//       for arguments and wrong for containers. This is the one item where the
//       honest answer is "std, plus a language question that is not yet
//       answerable": a `Box<Shape, M>` -- allocate, move the block in, keep
//       the fat pointer, call a `deinit` slot on destruction -- is writable
//       today except for the two things the language lacks, which are the move
//       ([P18]) and the destructor (FRICTION 22). Both are prerequisites, so
//       the proposal is really: decide those two, and this falls out of std
//       without new syntax.
//
//
// [P25] A VALUE MAY NOT BE DROPPED SILENTLY, and `_` already spells the
//       exception. `Store.remove` above discards three results -- two
//       `Option<C>` from `pop` and a bool from `Map.remove` -- and nothing in
//       the language says whether that is allowed, warned about, or an error.
//       It has to be decided, because `-> Result<T, E>` is the error channel
//       and an ignored Result is the one mistake that must not be easy:
//
//           xs.push(v)         fine, `push` projects `()`
//           xs.pop()           an error
//           _ := xs.pop()      "bind it to nothing", said out loud
//
//       The fourth use of one symbol, and no new one.
//
// [P26] COHERENCE: ONE ANONYMOUS INSTANCE PER (POLICY, TYPE), POOL IS
//       WHOLE-PROGRAM, DUPLICATES AND OVERLAPS ARE ERRORS AT THE DECLARATIONS,
//       AND THERE IS NO ORPHAN RULE.
//
//       unified.z has called this its biggest unanswered question since before
//       json.z. [P17] forced it: with arithmetic solver-dispatched, a second
//       anonymous `Add<Vec2, Vec2>` is a question about what `+` means in
//       every expression.
//
//       The pool must be global, not import-scoped, because `Map<slice<u8>, V>`
//       defaults its `Key` from it -- so an import-scoped pool would give one
//       spelling two layouts in two modules, which is the hazard NOTE 4
//       already refuses for shape satisfaction. Erroring at the USE site
//       instead is a dead end: the fix at a use site is to name the instance
//       you meant, and instances in conflict are anonymous by construction.
//       Most-specific-wins was rejected for the usual reason. An orphan rule
//       was rejected because extrinsic instances on foreign types is the whole
//       advantage policies hold over traits.
//
//       So it survives as a convention with teeth: an anonymous instance is a
//       claim on the global default, so publish one only for a policy or a
//       type you own; two libraries claiming one is a build error until one of
//       them names its instance. A loud failure in a rare case, in exchange
//       for extrinsic instances in every common one. See NOTE 8 in unified.z.
//
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THIS FILE SAYS ABOUT json.z's OPEN ITEMS
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   CONFIRMED, WORSE THAN STATED
//     FRICTION 8 (mutability) is not a documentation gap, it is the file's
//     top item -- see FRICTION 25. Interior pointers are the only mutable
//     handle, and they are invalidated by ordinary container operations.
//
//     FRICTION 7 (tag comparison) blocks a data structure, not a convenience:
//     no ordinal, no bitmask, no archetypes, no serialisation.
//
//     FRICTION 6 (arity families) becomes public API at a query interface.
//
//   CONFIRMED, AS STATED
//     FRICTION 2 (monomorphisation is viral) reappears in a SHAPE --
//     `System<M>` -- where the alias trick cannot reach, because a library
//     cannot name the application's allocator. The alias still fixes the
//     application layer, in one line, in a second unrelated program ([W13]).
//
//     FRICTION 10 (order independence) is required again: `World` names
//     `Signal`, declared two hundred lines later. Still unstated in unified.z.
//
//     FRICTION 12 (`_` does not reach projection position) recurs verbatim at
//     `Join2`, `Hit`, and `join2`.
//
//   DOWNGRADED
//     FRICTION 3 (no refutable match) barely showed up. The one-arm-plus-`_`
//     binding idiom absorbed almost every case here, including four in
//     `Combat.chase`. What is left is `is_dead`, which [P19] handles.
//
//   OPEN ITEM CLOSED -- COHERENCE.  RESOLVED -> [P26]
//     unified.z called two anonymous `-> Ord<u8>` in different files "the
//     biggest unanswered question". [P17] raised the stakes -- with arithmetic
//     solver-dispatched, a duplicate instance is a question about `a + b` in
//     every expression, not about a sort order -- and forced the decision.
//     One per (policy, type), global pool, no orphan rule.
//
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ONE-PARAGRAPH VERDICT
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// json.z built a tree and printed it, and found the language's missing
// vocabulary -- sums, `try`, `match`, `self`. This file MUTATES and STORES,
// and everything it found is downstream of those two verbs. The declaration
// form itself did not bend: `-> self` blocks as systems, generic stores erased
// to one shape, a policy as a state machine's formatter, all of it worked, and
// the shape/policy test answered a question (operators) it was never designed
// for, correctly, which is the best evidence in either file that the
// abstraction is real rather than tidy.
//
// What is missing is not more declaration syntax. It is that the language can
// say what a value IS and cannot yet say anything about WHERE IT LIVES, WHO
// MAY WRITE IT, or WHEN IT DIES. Places ([P22]), the move story ([P18]), the
// constructor body ([P21]) and ownership of erased values ([P20]) are four
// faces of that one gap, and mutability (FRICTION 25) is what it costs today.
// [P17] and [P24] are separate, cheap, and both REMOVE machinery: operators
// become policies the language already has, and an associated type becomes a
// hole whose type is `type`. Those two are ready now; the other four want one
// decision about memory, made once, on purpose.
// ============================================================================
